ovember 13 low (tied) of 80 since 1945.","t_rec":"Lancaster is setting record highs far faster than chance","p_rec":"If Lancaster's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Lancaster has set <strong>88 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 122 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">183 record highs</strong>, far more than the 122 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (73). Lancaster's all-time high is 115\u00b0F, on June 30, 2013.","t_chg":"Lancaster's days and nights both warmed. Nights warmed faster.","p_chg":"Across 73 complete years: Lancaster's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.50\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.39 to +0.61), about +4.0\u00b0F since 1945. Lancaster's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.19\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.01 to +0.36), about +1.5\u00b0F since 1945.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 10 of 12 months. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Lancaster's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.22 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 7.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 1983 (17.37 in); the driest, 1953 (1.26 in). Lancaster almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.2 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 16 daily record highs, 3 daily record lows"}}